\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Three commutants of the binary tetrahedral group}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{Let #{2T} be the group of 24 Hurwitz units in
\ref{fdip-007G}, and set #{\omega=(-1+i+j+k)/2}.
The rows of \ref{fgap-000K} partition #{2T} into the cosets
#{Q}, #{Q\omega} and #{Q\omega^2}, where
#{Q=\{\pm1,\pm i,\pm j,\pm k\}}.
Direct multiplication gives #{\omega^3=1} and
##{\omega i\omega^{-1}=k,\qquad
\omega k\omega^{-1}=j,\qquad
\omega j\omega^{-1}=i.}
Work in #{\operatorname{SL}(2,\mathbb F_3)}, with entries read in
#{\mathbb F_3}. Set
##{\iota=\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\eta=\begin{pmatrix}-1&1\\1&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\rho=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&-1\end{pmatrix},}
and put #{\kappa=\iota\eta}. Matrix multiplication gives
#{\iota^2=\eta^2=-I}, #{\iota\eta=-\eta\iota}, #{\rho^3=I}, and
##{\rho\iota\rho^{-1}=\kappa,\qquad
\rho\kappa\rho^{-1}=\eta,\qquad
\rho\eta\rho^{-1}=\iota.}
The 8 matrices #{\{\pm I,\pm\iota,\pm\eta,\pm\kappa\}} are
distinct, and the relations just given are the products of
#{i} and #{j} in #{Q}. Every
element of #{2T} is uniquely #{q\omega^r} with #{q\in Q} and
#{r\in\{0,1,2\}}, and
##{(q\omega^r)(q'\omega^s)
 =q\,(\omega^r q'\omega^{-r})\,\omega^{r+s}.}
Let #{\phi} send #{i,j,k} to #{\iota,\eta,\kappa} and send
#{\omega} to #{\rho}. The displayed conjugations say that
#{\phi(\omega q\omega^{-1})=\rho\phi(q)\rho^{-1}} on
#{\{i,j,k\}}, hence on #{Q}. Define
#{\phi(q\omega^r)=\phi(q)\rho^r}. This preserves the product
rule above, so #{\phi} is a homomorphism. The image
#{\phi(Q)} is a subgroup of order 8. If
#{\phi(q)\rho^r=I}, then #{\rho^r} lies in #{\phi(Q)}. The
order of #{\rho} is 3, so #{r=0} and #{q=1}. The kernel is
trivial.
A first column of a matrix in the codomain may be any of the
#{3^2-1=8} nonzero vectors, and #{w\mapsto\det(v,w)} takes
the value #{1} for exactly 3 choices of the second column.
The codomain therefore has order 24, and the homomorphism is
an isomorphism
##{2T\cong\operatorname{SL}(2,\mathbb F_3).}}

\p{\ref{fdip-0053} records the 7 irreducible characters, of
degrees #{1,1,1,2,2,2,3}, and the squares of the conjugacy
classes. Substitution of that table into the
[Frobenius-Schur indicator](fgap-0021)
#{|2T|^{-1}\sum_g\chi(g^2)} gives the numerators
#{24}, #{0}, #{0}, #{-24}, #{0}, #{0} and #{24} for
#{1}, #{\lambda}, #{\lambda^2}, #{\sigma}, #{\sigma\lambda},
#{\sigma\lambda^2} and #{\tau}. The indicators are
#{1,0,0,-1,0,0,1}. For the sample terms,
#{2+2-12-16=-24} is the numerator of #{\sigma}, and
#{3+3+18=24} is the numerator of #{\tau}.
\ref{fdip-0051} converts an indicator into a commutant.
The trivial representation and the degree-#{3} representation
#{\tau} have indicator #{1} and commutant #{\mathbb R}.
The defining representation #{\sigma} has indicator #{-1} and
commutant #{\mathbb H}. The other 4 irreducible characters
have indicator #{0}, and each conjugate pair has commutant
#{\mathbb C}. Thus each of #{\mathbb R}, #{\mathbb H} and
#{\mathbb C} occurs as #{\operatorname{End}_G(W)} for an
irreducible real representation #{W} of this group, and so
occurs as the scalar ring of a
[right multiplicity module](fdip-00KN)
#{\operatorname{Hom}_G(W,H)}.
The isomorphism is an identification of these finite groups.
It is not a physical symmetry assignment.}
